      "text": "To evaluate $\\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{3}\\right\\rfloor$, we can think of the value as a standard number. \n\nFirst, we can make a goal of converting the number to a positive one in order to \"see\" it. We know that $-\\frac{5}{3}$ comes closer to $-2.5$ than $-2$. In addition, we know that $-2.5$ is closer to $-3$ than it is to $-2$.\n\nWe can reach from $-3$ to $-2.5$ by moving towards $-3$ because it takes a smaller number to get closer to $-3$ than it does to move to $-2.5$. Thus, $\\left\\lfloor -2.5\\right\\rfloor = -3$.\n\nFinally, sending from $-3$ to $-5$ therefore shows that $\\left\\lfloor -\\frac{5}{3}\\right\\rfloor = -3$. Thus, the answer is \\boxed{-3}.",
